={∬}_{D}(3-(-2))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 48\\pi  \\\\  & =240\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45b042c5-5ebd-5d31-a6cc-baf65a7e1e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538163} {:answer/id #uuid "7f99a411-d07d-5e1d-8fc7-8e4bebe92bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538178} {:answer/id #uuid "a27bcbcc-285b-5d7f-9591-69f818793844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538187} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d2bb25-05b6-5674-817e-51dd14f03467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1848" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538190} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b8573b-d9b9-5a64-bb38-a1d9124d5ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f99a411-d07d-5e1d-8fc7-8e4bebe92bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538178} :answer-field/id #uuid "c84981b5-b8c1-55f7-8be1-ae3f5a997562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9687dcec-a46d-5800-a061-342d9450fae3" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a1b8a23b4fb0d081f9d88748735e02b3a173843e5c4a1a40e70e6ba4e7335ad.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-6y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-6))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}14\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (14\\cdot 8-{4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (112-16-4) \\\\  & =14\\cdot 92 \\\\  & =1288.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047f2b36-8964-5e1c-ba40-c92c874a8db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2350\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538152} {:answer/id #uuid "433dda26-4bb9-5114-b487-b99e5b406903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1925\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538162} {:answer/id #uuid "461804c5-46bb-5d96-af8e-d970cc58a5a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1800\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538164} {:answer/id #uuid "550a93f5-da6d-5738-b24c-952715ec8a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1700\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538168} {:answer/id #uuid "da4d1cc7-9202-56d5-99b4-8e92451ba91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1700\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "550a93f5-da6d-5738-b24c-952715ec8a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1700\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3960239-93b4-5860-9265-ba5ce587e842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa14acb3-297f-5aab-a401-2c7b97eae563" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2039c49121a2da30efa96b7d456a043aa3860bc19399e67385c3f3268f6c0f8.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=12x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}25\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {9}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (81\\pi-9\\pi-4\\pi) \\\\  & =25\\cdot 68\\pi \\\\  & =1700\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0efce4-e40a-5e17-b861-06b7b2120749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538160} {:answer/id #uuid "66452399-e90b-503f-8285-f8443359f389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "510\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538173} {:answer/id #uuid "70f313eb-7bf3-576e-aabf-93fe0ff7e116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538177} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a4a1df-b5f1-5dd4-9b8a-e7cd9b8d7028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-78\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538188} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf58509-b20b-5513-9bd9-8e1fec479d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70f313eb-7bf3-576e-aabf-93fe0ff7e116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538177} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b9efb0e-1e55-59e1-a247-642db1fe26b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf10ccdd-2f45-595c-a4ea-08a81d57feab" :question/problem "![](images/83/83357e8db109cd09ddcc51043caff588225edfd1b8f7b98dbb640a230ea7843d.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (49\\pi -36\\pi ) \\\\  & =78\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12575b41-8b98-5c1d-826e-16aacaf6e698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "760\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538153} {:answer/id #uuid "32f53203-099b-5874-889b-f83efd77d8a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-684\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538157} {:answer/id #uuid "861fadab-9825-5be8-8460-de34cdfe93f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "684\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538180} {:answer/id #uuid "92135252-c7a2-5a2e-b8bb-edf11e6375d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "855\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538183} {:answer/id #uuid "ef813cc5-c174-5a71-87e1-585aabb2bfa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1178\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "861fadab-9825-5be8-8460-de34cdfe93f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "684\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538180} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaf85fa-58f3-5f10-8de7-57b73f7c609c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e22aa2a8-a144-5ad0-98b1-7417fbf28999" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c92f6dbb417fb4ff7bff5442d50d57777c74a9072c13bbc614f333f76e04d7f2.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (49\\pi-4\\pi-9\\pi) \\\\  & =19\\cdot 36\\pi \\\\  & =684\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}]}
